00:45:08.740 --> 00:45:19.840
rebellious, full of fun. During the visit, Jack collapsed. The diagnosis was grim. A

00:45:19.840 --> 00:45:26.660
malfunctioning of the adrenal glands called Addison's disease. A doctor in London gave him

00:45:26.660 --> 00:45:29.880
a year to live. He crossed the Atlantic.

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:36.840
in a ship's hospital. The family told reporters waiting at the dock that Jack was suffering from

00:45:36.840 --> 00:45:45.960
a flare-up of malaria he'd contracted in the South Pacific. The good news was Joe Kennedy

00:45:45.960 --> 00:45:51.200
could afford the latest medicine, and there was a new treatment for Addison's, a potent

00:45:51.200 --> 00:45:59.410
cortisone-based steroid. It got him out of his deathbed and bought him time. He told one friend

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:01.520
hoped for maybe 10 more years.

00:46:05.200 --> 00:46:08.680
Eight months later, as he was beginning to regain his strength,

00:46:09.540 --> 00:46:13.270
28-year-old Kathleen died in a plane crash.

00:46:18.430 --> 00:46:19.190
He was devastated.

00:46:20.340 --> 00:46:21.480
He loved Kick.

00:46:21.700 --> 00:46:26.340
It was the first time in his life, really,

00:46:26.460 --> 00:46:29.460
and maybe the only time, where...

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:30.880
He didn't know what to do.

00:46:32.140 --> 00:46:33.660
It did make him a fatalist.

00:46:34.450 --> 00:46:40.070
He sent the signals of the kind of person who suspected that his time on earth was limited

00:46:40.070 --> 00:46:41.970
and that he had to make the most of it.

00:46:42.220 --> 00:46:43.360
He's lost a brother.

00:46:43.960 --> 00:46:45.460
He's lost his sister, Kick.

00:46:45.960 --> 00:46:48.100
He himself has been near death.

00:46:48.320 --> 00:46:53.740
There is a sense of mortality that lurks in there but also drives him, that he's got

00:46:53.740 --> 00:46:56.760
to accomplish something before he dies, that life is finite.

00:47:01.560 --> 00:47:08.230
In his second and third terms in Congress, John Kennedy seemed like a new man, a man in a hurry,

00:47:08.940 --> 00:47:11.500
always on the lookout for ways to distinguish himself.

00:47:13.700 --> 00:47:17.260
He exploited his experience in foreign affairs and defense policy,

00:47:19.220 --> 00:47:25.240
got himself invited to Senate hearings as an expert witness on the military readiness of our European allies,

00:47:27.410 --> 00:47:29.810
criticized President Harry Truman for...

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:34.540
adequate civil defense preparations in the wake of the Russians' first successful atom

00:47:34.540 --> 00:47:35.200
bomb test.

00:47:39.610 --> 00:47:45.260
What interested him was the question of the rising tensions with the Soviet Union, with

00:47:45.260 --> 00:47:51.020
the Civil War in China, with what was happening in Greece and Turkey, and how Harry Truman

00:47:51.020 --> 00:47:56.140
was responding to the dangers of flowing out of the Soviet Union in Eastern Europe.

00:48:06.840 --> 00:48:12.200
One of the great advantages of having a very rich father who's willing to spend whatever his sons ask for

00:48:12.200 --> 00:48:16.020
is that you can go on your own fact-finding missions.

00:48:16.140 --> 00:48:17.220
You can travel the world.

00:48:18.820 --> 00:48:21.380
And Jack does that twice in 1951.

00:48:24.160 --> 00:48:27.980
He talks to the journalists and the military men.

00:48:29.120 --> 00:48:29.980
He talks to world...

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:38.880
leaders and he talks to the opposition for seven weeks the young congressman traveled through

00:48:38.880 --> 00:48:50.250
israel iran pakistan india singapore thailand french indochina korea and japan he returned

00:48:50.250 --> 00:48:56.000
home with a new insight the united states was making few new friends in those places

00:48:56.000 --> 00:48:59.910
and losing old ones and jack kennedy

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:03.140
went on national radio and television to deliver the message.

00:49:03.360 --> 00:49:11.310
Our guest of the afternoon, ladies and gentlemen, will be Congressman John F. Kennedy of Boston.

00:49:12.160 --> 00:49:13.880
What do we do in Indochina then?

00:49:14.810 --> 00:49:19.170
Well, we tied ourselves completely with the French, and after all, the natives are anxious.

00:49:19.420 --> 00:49:22.600
You can never defeat the communist movement in Indochina until you get the support of

00:49:22.600 --> 00:49:25.640
the natives, and you won't get the support of the natives as long as they feel that the

00:49:25.640 --> 00:49:28.770
French are fighting the communists in order to hold their own power there.

00:49:29.160 --> 00:49:29.620
And I think we...

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:33.780
Kennedy had been invited to be on Meet the Press early on.

00:49:33.980 --> 00:49:36.820
He said, no, I don't want to do it, but why don't you invite my son?

00:49:38.100 --> 00:49:41.820
At the time, no junior congressman had ever been on.

00:49:41.960 --> 00:49:46.000
Only the biggest of the biggest stars in Washington were on Meet the Press.

00:49:46.660 --> 00:49:47.660
But Jack went on.

00:49:48.180 --> 00:49:52.460
Mr. Kennedy, when I was in Boston last week, I heard a good deal of talk about you.

00:49:52.970 --> 00:49:57.100
There were many who thought that you would be the Democratic nominee for the senatorship

00:49:57.800 --> 00:49:59.810
against Henry Cabot Lodge.

00:50:00.410 --> 00:50:01.370
Are you going to run?

00:50:05.620 --> 00:50:07.680
When Jack told his friends and his colleagues,

00:50:08.200 --> 00:50:11.120
I'm going to run for the Senate against Henry Cabot Lodge,

00:50:11.220 --> 00:50:14.780
they were unanimous in saying, don't do it.

00:50:15.200 --> 00:50:17.200
Nobody can beat a Lodge in Massachusetts.

00:50:17.840 --> 00:50:21.260
And Lodge is as handsome as you are, speaks as well,

00:50:21.440 --> 00:50:24.520
is as rich, and is a war hero.

00:50:24.820 --> 00:50:26.260
Don't even try.

00:50:27.340 --> 00:50:29.840
This is a very storied family.

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:37.130
As the old saying went, up in Boston, where the Lodges speak only to Cabots and the Cabots speak only to God.

00:50:37.410 --> 00:50:41.290
And so, can he defeat this Republican?

00:50:41.510 --> 00:50:44.590
And especially in 1952, it's a Republican year.

00:50:45.630 --> 00:50:47.950
His closest advisers told him, don't do it.

00:50:48.620 --> 00:50:49.840
This isn't your time.

00:50:50.520 --> 00:50:53.180
Maybe you should run, think about running for governor in Massachusetts.

00:50:53.400 --> 00:50:53.740
No, no, no.

00:50:53.960 --> 00:50:54.860
You want to run for Senate.

00:50:56.170 --> 00:50:59.970
This is a great state with a great past, and I believe in him.

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:05.950
even greater future. If elected to the United States Senate, with all of my energies and all

00:51:05.950 --> 00:51:12.200
of my resources, I will fight to secure that future for the people of this state and for the

00:51:12.200 --> 00:51:19.010
future of our country. And I know that it is not a one-way street. Whether 34-year-old John Kennedy

00:51:19.010 --> 00:51:25.980
was ready or not was an open question in the spring of 1952. And if elected to the Senate of

00:51:25.980 --> 00:51:29.530
the United States this November, I will fight for the New England industry.

00:51:32.120 --> 00:51:41.450
An uphill race against Henry Cabot Lodge was just the sort of challenge the Kennedys liked.

00:51:42.830 --> 00:51:47.010
Run, Jack, was the word at the family compound. You'll knock his block off.

00:51:49.350 --> 00:51:58.400
The most gleeful warrior in the Klan was Jack's younger brother, 26 years old, barely out of law school, hungry to prove himself.

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:04.050
Jack took a chance on brother Bobby and put him in charge of the campaign.

00:52:05.300 --> 00:52:08.460
Bobby Kennedy always wanted a role in the family, and he found one.

00:52:08.560 --> 00:52:09.520
He was the tough guy.

00:52:10.180 --> 00:52:12.490
He does not mince words.

00:52:12.810 --> 00:52:17.800
He's someone who is intent on winning this office for his brother,

00:52:17.860 --> 00:52:23.340
and if it means stepping on toes, hurting people's feelings, so be it.

00:52:24.420 --> 00:52:26.640
Bobby was able to come in and discipline the old hacks

00:52:26.640 --> 00:52:28.360
who were hanging around the campaign office,

00:52:28.710 --> 00:52:29.970
telling them to get off their duffs and...

00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:34.080
out and knock on a few doors, get rid of the ones who are truly useless.

00:52:34.580 --> 00:52:39.120
He passed around old Joe's money, put down the politicians they wanted to get rid of,

00:52:39.280 --> 00:52:40.880
made the deals that had to be made.

00:52:41.190 --> 00:52:45.420
Bobby's doing all the hard work, the dirty work, and it's liberating to Jack.

00:52:46.470 --> 00:52:53.140
Jack was able to float up there, quoting poetry and being a sort of young Lancelot.

00:52:54.600 --> 00:52:59.940
The Kennedy campaign was not shy to exploit the special appeal of the young congressman.

00:53:00.800 --> 00:53:02.720
the young bachelor congressman.

00:53:03.660 --> 00:53:07.420
His mother, his sisters, even Bobby's bride Ethel,

00:53:08.230 --> 00:53:10.430
fanned out into parlors across Massachusetts

00:53:10.430 --> 00:53:14.170
to sell Jack to a rising new block of voters.

00:53:20.380 --> 00:53:22.980
Women were not so involved as they are today, of course.

00:53:23.460 --> 00:53:26.160
And I think they were very struck by the fact

00:53:26.160 --> 00:53:29.250
that we were wandering around trying to get them

00:53:29.250 --> 00:53:29.970
to get out and vote.

00:53:30.120 --> 00:53:31.140
and get their friends to vote.

00:53:33.000 --> 00:53:35.670
Jack came at the end and gave a very good speech.

00:53:37.240 --> 00:53:38.600
People were very interested in him

00:53:38.600 --> 00:53:40.720
because they knew he was a hero and he was young.

00:53:41.660 --> 00:53:44.290
So they were very interested in how he did all this

00:53:44.290 --> 00:53:45.750
and what he looked like and everything.

00:53:46.720 --> 00:53:48.640
He was a very easy candidate to sell

00:53:48.640 --> 00:53:52.550
because he was good looking, he had enormous charm,

00:53:52.870 --> 00:53:55.520
he had a great sense of humor, and he was a real star.

00:54:04.540 --> 00:54:08.720
The polls showed Jack trailing the incumbent senator as election day neared,

00:54:09.850 --> 00:54:11.590
but he was working hard to close the gap.

00:54:13.720 --> 00:54:15.440
The demand of campaigning statewide,

00:54:15.480 --> 00:54:19.750
the distances traveled across the rough Massachusetts highways,

00:54:20.250 --> 00:54:23.370
was punishing, especially on Jack.

00:54:25.960 --> 00:54:29.480
His mental courage is so much superior to his physical strength.

00:54:30.000 --> 00:54:34.140
Joe Kennedy wrote, I sometimes wonder what the final result will be.

00:54:36.730 --> 00:54:39.090
Joe had another fear about his son's health.

00:54:40.060 --> 00:54:44.380
If Jack's Addison's disease became public, it could cost him the race,

00:54:45.280 --> 00:54:47.080
maybe even his political future.

00:54:48.380 --> 00:54:52.720
Jack was losing weight, but the Kennedys said, well, it's just the campaign.

00:54:53.870 --> 00:54:55.630
Jack Kennedy always had a suntan.

00:54:55.770 --> 00:54:57.930
Well, they said, well, he's out and he's getting a suntan.

00:54:58.050 --> 00:54:59.890
Actually, that was from his treatment.

00:55:00.150 --> 00:55:03.420
cortisone treatments for Addison's that darkened his skin.

00:55:04.000 --> 00:55:05.200
They covered all that up.

00:55:06.780 --> 00:55:09.440
A man who focuses on the word vigor

00:55:09.440 --> 00:55:13.620
in his public and private conversations

00:55:14.540 --> 00:55:17.840
must have in mind a sense of vitality,

00:55:18.840 --> 00:55:21.040
human vitality, as an ideal.

00:55:21.930 --> 00:55:25.880
Imagine the distance between the reality

00:55:25.880 --> 00:55:29.020
of his own physical troubles.

00:55:30.320 --> 00:55:39.250
and his ideal of the vigorous vital leader such a smart man would know this distance and understand

00:55:39.250 --> 00:55:45.930
the gap between reality of his own physical being and the image he wanted to project

00:55:45.930 --> 00:55:53.780
jack kept working down to the wire he still started his day earlier than his opponent

00:55:54.600 --> 00:55:59.960
traveled more miles campaigned later into the night he would not

00:56:00.000 --> 00:56:14.740
allow himself to lose for lack of effort. Senate races, Representative John F. Kennedy scores one

00:56:14.740 --> 00:56:20.200
of the few major Democratic victories, decisively defeating in a tough battle the Republican incumbent

00:56:20.200 --> 00:56:27.160
Henry Cabot Lawrence Jr. I'm glad it's over, aren't you, Bobby? I am, Jack. Okay.

00:56:42.760 --> 00:56:48.240
They were beginning to be seen together around town soon after he entered the Senate in 1953.

00:56:48.240 --> 00:56:53.890
Two dazzling stars in Washington's normally dull firmament.

00:56:55.490 --> 00:56:59.970
Jack Kennedy was 35 years old, the most sought-after bachelorette.

00:57:00.000 --> 00:57:07.780
in the capital. Jacqueline Bouvier was a shy 23-year-old beauty, the belle of Manhattan,

00:57:08.440 --> 00:57:15.500
East Hampton, and Newport. The couple had met at a dinner party two years earlier and had been

00:57:15.500 --> 00:57:23.800
warily circling one another ever since. She was engaged when she met him and she broke it

00:57:23.800 --> 00:57:29.620
off very quickly. She wrote in her diary that she had an intimation that Jack would have a profound

00:57:30.000 --> 00:57:34.080
and possibly disturbing effect on her life,

00:57:34.830 --> 00:57:35.850
but he was worth it.

00:57:37.920 --> 00:57:42.780
She once said to her sister that to her imagination

00:57:42.780 --> 00:57:46.420
was the most important thing she wanted to find in a man,

00:57:46.500 --> 00:57:48.660
and she said that's very difficult to find.

00:57:49.440 --> 00:57:52.790
From his standpoint, she was very different

00:57:52.790 --> 00:57:54.230
from the women that he'd known,

00:57:54.550 --> 00:57:56.980
which were primarily his own sisters,

00:57:57.930 --> 00:57:59.790
Eunice and Pat and Jean.

00:58:00.000 --> 00:58:03.620
were, what one of his friends said, tawny, cultish women.

00:58:03.940 --> 00:58:06.420
They were energetic and they were athletic

00:58:06.420 --> 00:58:07.800
and they were outspoken.

00:58:08.890 --> 00:58:13.270
Jackie, by contrast, was cerebral and soft-spoken,

00:58:13.270 --> 00:58:17.810
and they both had a kind of dry and sly wit.

00:58:22.000 --> 00:58:25.080
The romance was carried out largely in the public eye,

00:58:25.990 --> 00:58:28.550
and when Jackie agreed to marry the senator

00:58:28.550 --> 00:58:29.970
in the summer of 1950...

00:58:30.000 --> 00:58:33.570
the press was invited to share the joy.

00:58:41.700 --> 00:58:45.070
They were so beautiful, and they were so young.

00:58:46.940 --> 00:58:48.160
She was very stylish.

00:58:48.540 --> 00:58:50.280
Somebody in the New York Times wrote

00:58:50.280 --> 00:58:52.680
that she made the world safe for brunettes again.

00:58:55.300 --> 00:58:59.690
Jackie was smart, gorgeous, and although...

00:59:00.000 --> 00:59:06.420
Though she had not been born into a political family, she knew precisely what to say.

00:59:14.820 --> 00:59:20.230
Jack's closest friend, Lem Billings, actually warned her before they were married

00:59:20.230 --> 00:59:23.980
that she was going to be marrying a man who was known for his womanizing

00:59:23.980 --> 00:59:27.390
and that it was unlikely that he would stop.

00:59:28.020 --> 00:59:29.980
And she later said that instead of...

00:59:30.000 --> 00:59:36.140
of being put off by what Billings said, she actually viewed it as kind of a challenge.

00:59:42.440 --> 00:59:47.420
After the first year, Jackie was wandering around looking like the survivor of an airplane

00:59:47.420 --> 00:59:54.150
crash, a friend later remembered. Her new husband did not go out of his way to hide his dalliances

00:59:54.150 --> 00:59:59.940
from her. Jack Kennedy treated this as a matter of personal liberty and betrayed

01:00:00.000 --> 01:00:04.990
little guilt. He had this thing about him, said the man who introduced the Kennedys,

01:00:05.500 --> 01:00:10.920
which was not under control. And it wasn't just his womanizing that stunned Jackie.

01:00:12.180 --> 01:00:17.500
Politics was sort of my enemy, she confided. We had no home life whatsoever.

01:00:19.310 --> 01:00:21.790
Let's go and meet the newlywed. Are you there, Senator?

01:00:22.130 --> 01:00:23.270
Yes, right here, Mr. Morrow.

01:00:23.470 --> 01:00:24.130
Good evening, sir.

01:00:24.430 --> 01:00:24.790
Thank you.

01:00:24.990 --> 01:00:25.770
Good evening, Mrs. Kennedy.

01:00:26.080 --> 01:00:26.560
Good evening.

01:00:26.980 --> 01:00:29.900
I understand that the two of you had a very much publicized...

01:00:30.000 --> 01:00:33.700
Whatever her misgivings, Jackie Kennedy had married a politician,

01:00:33.700 --> 01:00:36.150
and she dutifully accepted her role.

01:00:37.190 --> 01:00:40.070
You first met the senator when you interviewed him?

01:00:40.600 --> 01:00:43.280
Well, I interviewed him shortly after I met him.

01:00:44.290 --> 01:00:46.290
Well, now, which requires the most diplomacy,

01:00:46.450 --> 01:00:48.630
to interview senators or to be married to one?

01:00:49.180 --> 01:00:52.300
Um, well...

01:00:52.930 --> 01:00:54.050
Being married to one, I guess.

01:00:58.450 --> 01:00:59.910
Over on Capitol Hill, however,

01:01:00.000 --> 01:01:02.960
However, the Kennedys' star power had less appeal.

01:01:03.950 --> 01:01:09.560
The young senator's way of being set Democratic leader Lyndon Johnson's teeth to grinding.

01:01:11.160 --> 01:01:16.950
Johnson looked at Jack as a person who picked and chose what he would like to do in the

01:01:16.950 --> 01:01:17.190
Senate.

01:01:17.940 --> 01:01:22.980
And the picking and choosing wasn't Johnson's idea of how the Senate ran, nor was it the

01:01:22.980 --> 01:01:25.970
idea of the other Southern moguls who charged.

01:01:26.750 --> 01:01:29.650
Kennedy was the troubadour who came and played before the banquet.

01:01:30.060 --> 01:01:32.120
left before the dishwashing began.

01:01:32.600 --> 01:01:36.180
And I think Lyndon talked about him in exactly those terms.

01:01:37.860 --> 01:01:40.120
Johnson says Kennedy was pathetic

01:01:40.120 --> 01:01:42.070
as a congressman and senator.

01:01:42.560 --> 01:01:44.640
He didn't know how to address the chair,

01:01:44.780 --> 01:01:46.580
by which he meant he didn't even know the rules.

01:01:48.820 --> 01:01:51.820
What irked Johnson was that he couldn't depend on the man.

01:01:52.650 --> 01:01:53.930
Kennedy was often absent.

01:01:54.220 --> 01:01:56.980
He ducked the controversial censure vote on Joe McCarthy.

01:01:58.600 --> 01:01:59.980
And Kennedy's insistent...

01:02:00.000 --> 01:02:03.120
on independence was maddening for the majority leader.

01:02:04.410 --> 01:02:06.850
Whether it was civil rights or labor legislation,

01:02:07.450 --> 01:02:10.130
Johnson couldn't count on the Democrat from Massachusetts

01:02:10.130 --> 01:02:11.700
to vote the party line.

01:02:15.190 --> 01:02:17.250
Lyndon Johnson could be cutting about Kennedy

01:02:17.250 --> 01:02:18.610
in front of fellow senators,

01:02:19.270 --> 01:02:21.050
said he looked like a victim of rickets,

01:02:21.480 --> 01:02:23.580
and joked about his puny little ankles.

01:02:25.340 --> 01:02:28.550
What Johnson didn't see was how tough Jack Kennedy

01:02:28.550 --> 01:02:29.980
had to be just to...

01:02:30.000 --> 01:02:36.920
get out of bed in the morning. By 1954, the drug he took to control his Addison's disease

01:02:36.920 --> 01:02:46.470
was eating away at his spine. It came to a point that in order for him to walk from his office to

01:02:46.470 --> 01:02:53.740
the Senate floor, he had to move across a marble floor, and it was so hard on his back, he needed

01:02:53.740 --> 01:02:59.980
crutches to allow him to put one foot in front of another without excruciation.

01:03:00.000 --> 01:03:05.130
And so what he decides to do is to have surgery

01:03:05.130 --> 01:03:08.670
even though it is a danger to his life.

01:03:10.170 --> 01:03:13.950
It requires the fusing of two large sections of the spine

01:03:13.950 --> 01:03:15.950
and a steel plate inserted there.

01:03:16.590 --> 01:03:19.410
What makes it risky is that he has Addison's disease,

01:03:19.530 --> 01:03:24.470
and Addison's disease leads to infections often during surgery.

01:03:25.840 --> 01:03:29.350
His father pleads with him, don't do this operation.

01:03:30.960 --> 01:03:33.420
And he holds out the example of Roosevelt.

01:03:33.760 --> 01:03:36.020
He said Roosevelt was president, and he was in a wheelchair.

01:03:36.280 --> 01:03:36.960
You can do it.

01:03:38.420 --> 01:03:45.500
Jack said to him, I'd rather be dead than be in a wheelchair hobbling around in crutches in pain the rest of my life.

01:03:46.640 --> 01:03:48.140
Jack goes ahead with the operation.

01:03:49.500 --> 01:03:51.520
Hours afterwards, an infection develops.

01:03:52.140 --> 01:03:53.040
Fever spikes.

01:03:53.870 --> 01:03:55.170
Last rites are performed.

01:03:57.000 --> 01:03:58.980
Jack pulls out Joe.

01:04:00.410 --> 01:04:02.960
hasn't flown to Palm Beach.

01:04:05.550 --> 01:04:08.190
He would suffer a series of setbacks in Florida.

01:04:09.420 --> 01:04:11.980
The eight-inch incision on his back would not close.

01:04:12.900 --> 01:04:15.790
He developed an abscess, needed a second surgery.

01:04:17.000 --> 01:04:19.800
The convalescence dragged on into 1955.

01:04:21.560 --> 01:04:26.340
Joe watches over him, hires his doctors, his nurses,

01:04:27.610 --> 01:04:29.970
converts a large part of their-

01:04:30.000 --> 01:04:35.800
Palm Beach House to a nursing facility and encourages Jack.

01:04:37.870 --> 01:04:42.450
The Kennedys told reporters that Jack's back problems were a result of war injuries.

01:04:43.260 --> 01:04:47.720
They did not disclose his ongoing need of steroids or his Addison's disease.

01:04:49.730 --> 01:04:55.860
Jack, meanwhile, began work on a second book, a series of essays about United States senators

01:04:55.860 --> 01:04:59.740
who had risked their political careers bucking convention and party.

01:05:00.090 --> 01:05:01.110
for a greater purpose.

01:05:02.510 --> 01:05:05.170
With the help of Library of Congress Research Files,

01:05:05.700 --> 01:05:08.160
Kennedy, his speechwriter Ted Sorensen,

01:05:08.440 --> 01:05:10.180
and a handful of Senate staffers

01:05:10.180 --> 01:05:12.500
produced Profiles in Courage.

01:05:14.200 --> 01:05:18.250
For seven, eight months, Jack recuperates,

01:05:18.710 --> 01:05:21.070
and only after a lengthy period

01:05:21.580 --> 01:05:24.490
is he able to return to the Senate.

01:05:24.610 --> 01:05:27.050
I feel to be back.

01:05:27.370 --> 01:05:29.670
Well, I'm glad to be back here and have a chance to...

01:05:30.350 --> 01:05:34.100
take part in what's going on. My wife is there.

01:05:36.480 --> 01:05:42.350
He returns in pain, and he will remain in pain for the rest of his life.

01:05:53.600 --> 01:05:59.980
Now my privilege to present to this convention as a candidate for...

01:06:00.000 --> 01:06:07.060
President of the United States, the name of the man uniquely qualified by virtue of his

01:06:07.060 --> 01:06:09.880
compassion, his conscience, and his courage.

01:06:10.230 --> 01:06:16.340
The 1956 Democratic presidential nominee, Adlai Stevenson, gave his party's youngest

01:06:16.340 --> 01:06:23.540
senator a starring role at the convention, the official nominating speech, and his performance

01:06:23.540 --> 01:06:26.380
helped ignite a Kennedy for Vice President boom.

01:06:32.240 --> 01:06:34.000
How would you like to be vice president with him?

01:06:34.460 --> 01:06:37.020
Well, I'd be honored the court is chosen,

01:06:37.180 --> 01:06:39.180
but I've always had my doubts whether I'd ever be chosen.

01:06:40.490 --> 01:06:43.130
He wasn't sure he even wanted a place on the ticket.

01:06:44.140 --> 01:06:46.200
Joe Kennedy had counseled him to steer clear,

01:06:47.280 --> 01:06:49.600
but Stevenson threw the choice to a floor vote,

01:06:50.200 --> 01:06:53.800
and Jack Kennedy had a hard time backing down from a challenge,

01:06:54.790 --> 01:06:58.910
even against the better-known and esteemed senator Estes Kefauver.

01:07:01.210 --> 01:07:03.030
Jack Kennedy liked his chances,

01:07:03.070 --> 01:07:06.050
and he liked the feeling on the convention floor.

01:07:07.510 --> 01:07:09.950
The delegates took his candidacy seriously.

01:07:12.250 --> 01:07:14.610
This whole thing lasted like 24 hours

01:07:14.890 --> 01:07:17.370
before the vice-presidential balloting,

01:07:18.210 --> 01:07:20.350
and Kennedy makes a real try for it.

01:07:20.630 --> 01:07:26.460
Headed, bold sailor!

01:07:31.210 --> 01:07:33.990
the next vice president of the United States,

01:07:34.280 --> 01:07:35.980
John Kennedy of Massachusetts.

01:07:36.220 --> 01:07:40.530
For a moment, it seems actually like he's going to win.

01:07:41.950 --> 01:07:43.890
But Kefauver beats him.

01:07:45.060 --> 01:07:48.080
He has to make a contestant speech to Kefauver.

01:07:48.550 --> 01:07:52.890
When he gets up there, he's facing a sea of Kefauver signs.

01:07:52.990 --> 01:07:54.370
They're all waving in his face.

01:07:54.410 --> 01:07:57.120
And you look at Kennedy, he's always immaculate.

01:07:57.760 --> 01:07:59.360
At this moment, he's not immaculate.

01:08:01.760 --> 01:08:07.480
Ladies and gentlemen, ladies and gentlemen of this convention.

01:08:07.780 --> 01:08:11.300
In fact, one point of the collar of his shirt is sticking out.

01:08:12.030 --> 01:08:18.450
And as he's talking, if you watch his hands, he has the gavel in his hands and he restlessly turns it around.

01:08:19.650 --> 01:08:25.400
You saw a young man in defeat and you also see someone who covers it up so well.

01:08:26.180 --> 01:08:29.740
I hope that this convention will make F.B.C. fall for his nomination.

01:08:30.060 --> 01:08:42.710
unanimous thank you jack was very depressed very upset and bobby was there and he couldn't cheer

01:08:42.710 --> 01:08:51.600
him up and he said let's call dad so i remember when we all went to call dad and he said congratulations

01:08:52.520 --> 01:08:57.100
he said that's the best thing that ever happened to you that was magnificent i don't know how you

01:08:57.100 --> 01:08:59.120
did that was absolutely great

01:09:00.000 --> 01:09:04.270
said, Adelaide Stevenson is going nowhere. He said, he's going nowhere. And that's,

01:09:04.330 --> 01:09:08.430
Keith Foster's going nowhere. So you just pulled it off. And I can't tell you how

01:09:08.840 --> 01:09:18.720
wonderful that was. And Jack came out beaming, beaming. Joe Kennedy knew what he was talking

01:09:18.720 --> 01:09:24.870
about. Stevenson lost big to Eisenhower, which made the governor a two-time loser

01:09:24.870 --> 01:09:28.950
and left the Democratic nomination wide open next time round.

01:09:32.940 --> 01:09:38.700
Jack Kennedy understood the obstacles to winning the presidency in 1960, and they were not small.

01:09:40.350 --> 01:09:42.790
He was younger than anybody ever elected to the office.

01:09:44.380 --> 01:09:46.720
He had few legislative achievements to run on.

01:09:49.920 --> 01:09:51.900
And then, too, there was his religion.

01:09:53.560 --> 01:09:59.720
In 1957, a quarter of the electorate still said they were unwilling to vote for a Catholic.

01:10:00.060 --> 01:10:08.600
There was a fear across the land that Catholics would be controlled by the Pope,

01:10:08.700 --> 01:10:12.620
that they couldn't think on their own, and therefore they weren't really Americans

01:10:12.620 --> 01:10:14.850
in the way that Protestants were.

01:10:16.400 --> 01:10:19.680
Some in the party argued the country would change in time,

01:10:20.270 --> 01:10:23.300
that he was still a young man, that he could wait it out.

01:10:24.980 --> 01:10:27.090
Jack Kennedy thought otherwise.

01:10:28.220 --> 01:10:29.980
His star turn at the 1950s...

01:10:31.020 --> 01:10:34.200
meant he would be taken seriously in 1960.

01:10:35.560 --> 01:10:37.780
He was not going to let this moment pass.

01:10:37.920 --> 01:10:43.680
I want to be sure that we haven't lost something important in this country,

01:10:44.090 --> 01:10:45.390
that we haven't gone soft.

01:10:45.550 --> 01:10:49.090
He had campaigned across the country for Stevenson in 1956.

01:10:49.570 --> 01:10:51.850
That we just look to our own private interests.

01:10:51.990 --> 01:10:54.610
Let us cut the budget and let us save on foreign aid.

01:10:54.730 --> 01:10:57.430
And with his speechwriter Ted Sorensen riding shotgun,

01:10:57.770 --> 01:10:59.790
he just kept going in 1956.

01:11:00.000 --> 01:11:07.280
The reason the communists attack us is because they know when the United States fails, the cause of freedom fails.

01:11:08.060 --> 01:11:12.010
There were county chairmen to meet in every state, delegates to woo.

01:11:14.630 --> 01:11:18.040
Jackie was pregnant most of that year, and nervously so.

01:11:19.310 --> 01:11:23.080
She'd already had one miscarriage and delivered a stillborn daughter.

01:11:24.300 --> 01:11:26.160
But her husband rarely stopped traveling.

01:11:28.560 --> 01:11:29.980
When Kennedy's new back special...

01:11:30.000 --> 01:11:34.490
went to Palm Beach for a consultation, she, too, got the program.

01:11:35.850 --> 01:11:38.950
She comes down to this huge map of the United States

01:11:38.950 --> 01:11:42.510
where his father and he are plotting out, you know, his next trips.

01:11:43.150 --> 01:11:45.150
He's traveling all around the United States

01:11:45.150 --> 01:11:46.990
trying to make contact with politicians.

01:11:47.920 --> 01:11:51.900
And she says, well, you know, to do this, you need periods of rest.

01:11:52.760 --> 01:11:55.970
And he says, well, there's no time for rest.

01:11:56.010 --> 01:11:58.010
And she says, well, you have to change the schedule.

01:11:58.530 --> 01:11:59.450
And he said...

01:12:00.000 --> 01:12:01.620
The schedule will not be changed.

01:12:08.330 --> 01:12:11.710
When he was in Washington, Kennedy was always on the lookout

01:12:11.710 --> 01:12:13.670
for ways to take a stand apart

01:12:13.670 --> 01:12:16.060
from the other would-be presidents in the Senate.

01:12:17.090 --> 01:12:19.110
Stuart Symington, Hubert Humphrey,

01:12:19.530 --> 01:12:22.910
and, above all, the majority leader, Lyndon Johnson.

01:12:23.980 --> 01:12:26.260
This is a strike-breaking, union-busting bill.

01:12:26.480 --> 01:12:29.960
Mr. Hopper, this bill is not a strike-breaking, union-busting bill.

01:12:30.000 --> 01:12:34.960
Bill, best argument I know for it, your testimony here this afternoon, your complete indifference

01:12:34.960 --> 01:12:35.580
to the fact that...

01:12:35.580 --> 01:12:40.580
He dabbled in domestic issues where he saw opportunity, like in the nationally televised

01:12:40.580 --> 01:12:42.540
hearings into racketeering in the labor unions.

01:12:42.540 --> 01:12:44.680
An honest answer might tend to incriminate them.

01:12:44.780 --> 01:12:47.760
Your complete indifference to it, I think, makes this bill a sentence.

01:12:48.580 --> 01:12:52.480
His chief interest and his focus remained foreign affairs.

01:12:53.700 --> 01:12:59.710
His father even managed to talk Lyndon Johnson into giving Jack a coveted slot on the Senate

01:12:59.710 --> 01:12:59.970
foreign affairs.

01:13:00.000 --> 01:13:04.620
relations committee when Kennedy said that he would become chairman of the

01:13:04.620 --> 01:13:08.320
African subcommittee in the Senate Foreign Relations Committee he sort of

01:13:08.320 --> 01:13:11.400
got a commitment that it would never have to meet and Johnson thought that

01:13:11.400 --> 01:13:21.930
was typical of the kind of committee that Kennedy would like to run like this

01:13:21.930 --> 01:13:26.290
are taking place daily all over Algeria as French colonial troops round up

01:13:26.290 --> 01:13:29.970
natives by the thousands in a desperate attempt to halt the guerrilla reign of

01:13:30.000 --> 01:13:32.840
terror that has spread the length and breadth of the colony.

01:13:33.860 --> 01:13:39.770
The bloody escalation of the three-year-old war for independence in Algeria gave Senator

01:13:39.770 --> 01:13:45.000
Kennedy a shot at the spotlight, and one that played to his long-held interest in foreign

01:13:45.000 --> 01:13:45.500
policy.

01:13:47.790 --> 01:13:55.920
He identifies himself with a kind of anti-colonial posture, with the idea that the United States

01:13:55.920 --> 01:13:59.830
is locked in a contest with the Soviet Union.

01:14:00.000 --> 01:14:06.190
for hearts and minds in the third world, in the developing world, in Africa, in Asia, in Latin America.

01:14:06.350 --> 01:14:10.380
And he sees Algeria as the case study of the time.

01:14:11.510 --> 01:14:18.280
I'm concerned today that we are failing to meet the challenge of imperialism on both counts, both East and West,

01:14:18.990 --> 01:14:23.300
and thus failing in our responsibilities to the free world and to ourselves.

01:14:23.560 --> 01:14:29.900
What Kennedy was saying was, we know that French imperialism is going to die.

01:14:31.280 --> 01:14:34.500
The question is, are we going to be on the right side or the wrong side of history?

01:14:35.130 --> 01:14:38.800
If we make a choice now, we can help shape the outcome.

01:14:39.560 --> 01:14:42.320
If we align ourselves with Paris until the bitter end,

01:14:42.720 --> 01:14:47.150
the new generation of leaders in Algeria will remember that and won't talk to us.

01:14:48.320 --> 01:14:54.670
I'm introducing a resolution which I believe outlines the best hopes of peace and a settlement in Algeria.

01:14:55.560 --> 01:14:59.980
Dean Acheson, the former Secretary of State, came out saying this speech...

01:15:00.000 --> 01:15:04.060
was the irresponsible utterings of a juvenile senator

01:15:04.060 --> 01:15:07.360
because it was throwing aside our alliance

01:15:07.360 --> 01:15:10.340
with Portugal and France and England

01:15:10.340 --> 01:15:14.470
in support of Africa and Asia, et cetera.

01:15:16.490 --> 01:15:18.550
France was a NATO ally of ours in Europe.

01:15:19.220 --> 01:15:21.860
Were we going to abandon our ally

01:15:21.860 --> 01:15:25.270
for the sake of a group of revolutionaries

01:15:25.270 --> 01:15:26.430
who might turn out to be communist?

01:15:27.330 --> 01:15:28.750
Kennedy said, yeah, you take that chance

01:15:28.750 --> 01:15:29.890
because you want to vote.

01:15:30.000 --> 01:15:31.140
with the future, not with the past.

01:15:32.470 --> 01:15:35.570
Senator, what do you feel is the single most critical issue

01:15:35.570 --> 01:15:37.230
facing the Congress at this time?

01:15:37.430 --> 01:15:39.450
Well, I think it's the same issue

01:15:39.450 --> 01:15:40.950
which has been facing us for 10 years,

01:15:40.990 --> 01:15:42.830
and that's our relations with the Soviet Union

01:15:42.830 --> 01:15:45.060
and this question of war and peace,

01:15:45.120 --> 01:15:49.200
and also the question of whether the uncommitted countries,

01:15:49.320 --> 01:15:50.520
the Middle East, Africa, and Asia,

01:15:50.620 --> 01:15:52.700
will move to the communist bloc or our own,

01:15:53.160 --> 01:15:56.180
turn the balance of power for us or against us.

01:15:56.550 --> 01:15:58.410
And that's obviously the most important issue today,

01:15:58.490 --> 01:15:59.960
and will be during...

01:16:00.000 --> 01:16:23.920
i think our lifetime when sputnik went up by the russians the surprise could not have been greater

01:16:23.920 --> 01:16:29.970
how did they get ahead of us in the rush

01:16:30.000 --> 01:16:36.220
claimed they invented everything, the car, the plane, penicillin, whatever it was. The Russians

01:16:36.220 --> 01:16:42.990
would always say, oh no, we had that first. This they had first, and they proved it. The October

01:16:42.990 --> 01:16:51.210
1957 launch of Sputnik, a 184-pound beach ball-sized satellite, spurred an instant jump in

01:16:51.210 --> 01:16:57.810
Cold War hysteria, and not without reason. If the Soviets were able to launch a satellite into space,

01:16:58.410 --> 01:16:59.970
could they also reach the U.S.?

01:17:00.000 --> 01:17:01.800
with nuclear-armed missiles.

01:17:04.550 --> 01:17:07.530
U.S. Air Force bombers went on 24-hour alert.

01:17:09.090 --> 01:17:13.430
The Eisenhower administration began sending extra planes into Soviet airspace

01:17:13.430 --> 01:17:19.170
just to remind Soviet Premier Nikita Khrushchev who had the upper hand in bombers.

01:17:23.140 --> 01:17:27.710
When President Eisenhower pursued more and more potent nuclear warheads

01:17:27.710 --> 01:17:29.960
for the U.S. arsenal, the Soviets...

01:17:30.000 --> 01:17:38.280
answered. Six in ten Americans believed nuclear war was imminent and would be catastrophic.

01:17:47.860 --> 01:17:55.890
Kennedy appeared unruffled by the rising dangers of the Cold War. He increased his travel schedule,

01:17:56.360 --> 01:17:59.970
running harder state to state. The list of delegates who had...

01:18:00.000 --> 01:18:03.190
courted his vice-presidential candidacy tucked in his pocket.

01:18:06.840 --> 01:18:08.680
Jack Kennedy could learn on the run.

01:18:08.960 --> 01:18:10.920
So he's traveling around the country,

01:18:11.070 --> 01:18:13.570
and he's seeing that politics is changing.

01:18:14.110 --> 01:18:17.370
He's learning that the power isn't back in Washington anymore.

01:18:17.510 --> 01:18:20.190
The power is with these younger people in the States,

01:18:20.190 --> 01:18:21.590
if he can just line them up.

01:18:22.010 --> 01:18:26.340
For him, he's learning that the old party machinery doesn't work.

01:18:27.270 --> 01:18:29.270
He knows that he's got the money.

01:18:30.000 --> 01:18:37.020
mount an independent campaign, that he's got the charisma without the help of any party

01:18:37.020 --> 01:18:44.240
bigwigs or any party establishment to get his photo on the cover of Time and the Saturday

01:18:44.240 --> 01:18:45.360
Evening Post and look.

01:18:46.890 --> 01:18:53.400
He begins a change in American politics that is quite significant.

01:18:53.800 --> 01:18:59.960
He signals the beginning of a move from party dominance and...

01:19:00.000 --> 01:19:08.480
party candidates, to the individual, to the personality, who can speak to the people, not

01:19:08.530 --> 01:19:12.890
through the party, but through television and the mass media directly.

01:19:15.070 --> 01:19:20.600
Pleasure to have you here, and I want you to meet my daughter Caroline and my wife Jackie.

01:19:22.540 --> 01:19:26.220
Joe Kennedy was nudging every editor he knew in 1959.

01:19:27.260 --> 01:19:28.380
You want to sell magazines?

01:19:28.380 --> 01:19:29.980
Put Jack and Jack.

01:19:30.000 --> 01:19:30.940
Jackie on your cover.

01:19:33.120 --> 01:19:36.440
Jackie Kennedy chafed at the requirement of public display.

01:19:37.660 --> 01:19:40.300
But when the photographers showed up on the Kennedy doorstep,

01:19:41.060 --> 01:19:42.120
she did not disappoint.

01:19:43.800 --> 01:19:45.820
It used to drive Humphrey nuts because he said,

01:19:45.840 --> 01:19:48.600
every time I go into the supermarket to go shopping for Muriel,

01:19:48.840 --> 01:19:51.570
I see Red Book or I see Good Housekeeping

01:19:51.570 --> 01:19:53.050
or I see Saturday Evening Post,

01:19:53.130 --> 01:19:55.920
all with the Kennedys smiling at me on the cover.

01:19:57.360 --> 01:19:59.820
Senator, when are you going to drop this public?

01:20:00.000 --> 01:20:05.400
pretense of non-candidacy and frankly admit that you already are seeking the Democratic

01:20:05.400 --> 01:20:09.820
presidential nomination in 1960. Well, Mr. Lawrence, I think there's an appropriate time

01:20:09.820 --> 01:20:14.040
for anyone to make a decision and a final announcement as to whether he's going to be a

01:20:14.040 --> 01:20:29.120
candidate. It seemed to me that there was a sort of...

01:20:30.000 --> 01:20:31.720
a perpetual half-smile on his face.

01:20:32.920 --> 01:20:34.960
There was a sense of joy about what he was doing,

01:20:35.120 --> 01:20:36.360
that he loved what he was doing.

01:20:37.420 --> 01:20:40.430
He's only 43 years old, and a woman says to him,

01:20:40.470 --> 01:20:42.550
young man, it's too soon, and he says,

01:20:42.670 --> 01:20:44.230
no, ma'am, this is my time.

01:20:44.620 --> 01:20:47.370
I am today announcing my candidacy

01:20:47.370 --> 01:20:49.750
for the presidency of the United States.

01:20:50.720 --> 01:20:52.860
The presidency is the most powerful office

01:20:52.880 --> 01:20:54.000
in the free world.

01:20:54.780 --> 01:20:57.560
Through its leadership can come a more vital life

01:20:57.560 --> 01:20:59.070
for all of our people.

01:21:00.730 --> 01:21:04.870
Kennedy officially announced his candidacy in January of 1960.

01:21:06.380 --> 01:21:11.560
Political oddsmakers put his chances well below Senators Symington, Humphrey, and Johnson.

01:21:16.220 --> 01:21:19.990
And if the old rules applied, Kennedy was surely in trouble.

01:21:20.930 --> 01:21:23.970
The well-worn path to the Democratic presidential nomination

01:21:23.970 --> 01:21:29.700
went through the state party chairman and the big city bosses, who still thought...

01:21:30.000 --> 01:21:31.920
They could keep their delegations in line.

01:21:33.470 --> 01:21:37.410
But Kennedy already had a handful of key players in every state

01:21:37.410 --> 01:21:41.530
and a way to show himself a winner, the primaries.

01:21:44.360 --> 01:21:49.100
The few state primaries were regarded as side events before 1960.

01:21:49.940 --> 01:21:54.690
Fine for junior senators like Jack Kennedy, but not worthy of serious candidates.

01:21:55.850 --> 01:21:57.990
Lyndon Johnson sat them out that year.

01:22:00.000 --> 01:22:03.000
stayed in the Senate, stayed as majority leader,

01:22:03.120 --> 01:22:05.020
told everybody else who was leaving town

01:22:05.020 --> 01:22:06.340
that they should be ashamed of themselves

01:22:06.340 --> 01:22:08.500
and they should be back legislating, not speaking.

01:22:10.020 --> 01:22:13.470
Johnson's supposition is that he's earned the nomination

01:22:13.470 --> 01:22:16.760
by dint of his role as Senate Majority Leader.

01:22:17.060 --> 01:22:20.960
He has very good relations with various party bosses

01:22:20.960 --> 01:22:22.460
across the country,

01:22:22.460 --> 01:22:24.620
and that Jack Kennedy is an upstart.

01:22:25.040 --> 01:22:27.400
Who is this kid who's trying to displace me

01:22:27.400 --> 01:22:28.480
and take the nomination?

01:22:28.480 --> 01:22:29.540
I deserve it.

01:22:32.520 --> 01:22:35.580
Senator Humphrey just stopped by to say hello, if that's it.

01:22:36.150 --> 01:22:38.910
The most important early primary was in Wisconsin,

01:22:39.480 --> 01:22:41.180
where Kennedy had a real opponent,

01:22:41.800 --> 01:22:45.490
the popular senator from neighboring Minnesota, Hubert Humphrey.

01:22:47.170 --> 01:22:48.510
Campaign, can I have that?

01:22:49.150 --> 01:22:50.270
I'm running short.

01:22:50.660 --> 01:22:52.620
You should realize that you are voting

01:22:52.620 --> 01:22:55.560
for the most important individual in the entire free world.

01:22:56.090 --> 01:22:59.980
He cast Humphrey as the establishment candidate and ran...

01:23:00.000 --> 01:23:05.620
against the party bosses, and he cast himself as the underdog,

01:23:06.240 --> 01:23:09.340
in spite of a huge advantage in money and television exposure

01:23:09.340 --> 01:23:12.490
and having celebrity backers like Frank Sinatra.

01:23:17.620 --> 01:23:22.950
Double Y, Jack's the nation's favorite guy.

01:23:23.510 --> 01:23:29.920
Everyone wants to back, Jack, Jack is on the right track, come on.

01:23:30.060 --> 01:23:44.310
And vote for Kennedy, vote for Kennedy, keep America scoony, just go to Canada, go to Canada.

01:23:51.800 --> 01:23:57.720
Good evening. Good evening. All these election nights are, it's a very interesting evening.

01:23:58.530 --> 01:23:59.910
He knew on election day.

01:24:00.000 --> 01:24:06.430
was going to win. But as the results came in and his margin was narrower than he'd expected,

01:24:07.190 --> 01:24:13.830
Kennedy began to understand there would be a caveat. The party elders could argue that his

01:24:13.830 --> 01:24:19.450
victory in Wisconsin owed to his overwhelming margin in the state's large block of big-city

01:24:19.450 --> 01:24:27.040
Catholic voters, as if his religion had been an unfair advantage. He understands this is not

01:24:27.040 --> 01:24:29.980
enough. If he's going to win that nomination, he has to commit.

01:24:30.000 --> 01:24:32.360
in the Democratic Party and around the country

01:24:32.360 --> 01:24:34.470
that he can win Protestant votes,

01:24:34.590 --> 01:24:37.300
that he's more than just a Catholic candidate.

01:24:38.420 --> 01:24:40.500
His sister, after the victory in Wisconsin,

01:24:40.720 --> 01:24:41.900
says to him, well, what does it mean?

01:24:42.080 --> 01:24:45.020
He says it means we've got to go on to West Virginia.

01:24:45.830 --> 01:24:49.290
West Virginia is a state with 97% Protestant population.

01:25:00.570 --> 01:25:03.230
Humphrey started with a 20-point lead in West Virginia

01:25:03.230 --> 01:25:07.330
and the backing of the state's popular senator, Robert Byrd.

01:25:08.040 --> 01:25:10.500
He also got a new campaign theme song,

01:25:11.220 --> 01:25:12.760
the anti-Catholic dog whistle,

01:25:13.460 --> 01:25:15.040
Give Me That Old-Time Religion.

01:25:19.150 --> 01:25:20.590
Kennedy's answered in kind.

01:25:21.590 --> 01:25:23.290
Joe blanketed the state with money,

01:25:23.760 --> 01:25:26.020
buying the support of crucial local bosses.

01:25:27.030 --> 01:25:29.710
Bobby recruited Franklin Delano Roosevelt Jr.

01:25:30.250 --> 01:25:34.110
to allege that Humphrey had shirked his military duty in World War II.

01:25:34.880 --> 01:25:38.380
Jack Kennedy and I served in the United States Navy for five years.

01:25:38.960 --> 01:25:45.480
And John Kennedy then dismisses this as a terrible thing to have been said about Hubert.

01:25:45.660 --> 01:25:47.640
And he keeps going around the state saying,

01:25:47.740 --> 01:25:51.100
it's a terrible thing to say that Hubert's a draft dodger, a terrible thing,

01:25:51.240 --> 01:25:55.780
until it fastens itself on people's minds that Hubert maybe was a draft dodger.

01:25:57.420 --> 01:25:59.980
Kennedy left West Virginia on May 11th...

01:26:00.000 --> 01:26:07.260
1960 with a win. He got in his private plane, outfitted to carry staff and press, a first in

01:26:07.260 --> 01:26:13.860
presidential campaigns, and flew off to primaries in Maryland and then Oregon to pile up more

01:26:13.860 --> 01:26:21.200
delegates to take to the nominating convention that July. Jack Kennedy is going around the

01:26:21.200 --> 01:26:27.160
country. He's showing the country what he is, this charming, incredible, adept campaigner.

01:26:27.950 --> 01:26:29.970
The New York Times says the collider...

01:26:30.000 --> 01:26:35.680
sound of a bandwagon is being heard in the Democratic Party. All of a sudden,

01:26:35.820 --> 01:26:40.450
Lyndon Johnson wakes up. Senator Jack Kennedy of Massachusetts has won every

01:26:40.450 --> 01:26:44.580
primary in which he's entered. He's won him in a breeze. Does this entitle him?

01:26:44.580 --> 01:26:47.200
Senator Kennedy is a very effective and able young man.

01:26:47.580 --> 01:26:50.520
Does this entitle him to the Democratic presidential nomination?

01:26:51.410 --> 01:26:56.350
Well, I wouldn't think that we would want to nominate our president on the basis of

01:26:56.350 --> 01:26:59.920
what four states or five states or six states or eight.

01:27:00.000 --> 01:27:07.280
states might say in a limited primary system where only a few people participate, I think

01:27:07.280 --> 01:27:14.000
Senator Kennedy is. By the time he got around to announcing his candidacy for the Democratic

01:27:14.000 --> 01:27:20.290
presidential nomination, just a week before the party convention in Los Angeles, Johnson needed

01:27:20.290 --> 01:27:28.130
a miracle. So he pulled out his last best hope. He sent a private investigator to dig up Kennedy's

01:27:28.130 --> 01:27:28.710
health records.

01:27:31.630 --> 01:27:34.430
They get to the Democratic convention in Los Angeles,

01:27:34.580 --> 01:27:39.240
and Johnson unleashes his aide, a man named John Conley,

01:27:39.930 --> 01:27:44.930
and Conley will issue a story about Kennedy's Addison's disease,

01:27:45.230 --> 01:27:51.520
raising the question of whether Kennedy is physically capable of serving as president.

01:27:55.520 --> 01:27:59.760
That Jack Kennedy suffered from Addison's disease was a fact beyond dispute.

01:28:00.830 --> 01:28:02.760
But the Kennedys disputed it.

01:28:03.670 --> 01:28:07.520
John F. Kennedy does not now, nor has he ever had an ailment,

01:28:07.650 --> 01:28:11.540
described classically as Addison's disease, Bobby claimed.

01:28:14.060 --> 01:28:18.300
The Addison story didn't stick, but Johnson kept fighting anyway.

01:28:19.220 --> 01:28:22.200
He still couldn't believe Jack Kennedy, of all people,

01:28:22.770 --> 01:28:24.670
could take the nomination away from him.

01:28:30.000 --> 01:28:39.560
had 24 hour session. Six days and nights I had to deliver a quorum of 51 men on a moment's

01:28:39.560 --> 01:28:46.470
notice to keep the Senate in session to get any bill at all. And I'm proud to tell you

01:28:46.470 --> 01:28:52.690
that on those 50 quorum calls, Lyndon Johnson answered every one of them.

01:29:10.080 --> 01:29:18.200
Some men who would be president on a civil rights platform answered none.

01:29:19.940 --> 01:29:23.100
Let me just say that I appreciate what Senator Johnson had to say.

01:29:23.620 --> 01:29:29.530
He made some general references to perhaps the shortcomings of other presidential candidates.

01:29:30.000 --> 01:29:34.320
He was not specific. I assume he was talking about some of the other candidates and not about me.

01:29:35.790 --> 01:29:39.230
Kennedy parried Johnson with the grace of a sure winner.

01:29:40.740 --> 01:29:46.470
Bobby, meanwhile, was working the phones, keeping a white-knuckle grip on his brother's committed delegates.

01:29:47.920 --> 01:29:51.520
He knew Johnson operatives were still trying to peel them away.

01:29:53.990 --> 01:29:59.720
Seven and one-half votes for Johnson.

01:30:01.690 --> 01:30:04.650
Thirty-three and one-half votes for Kennedy.

01:30:06.030 --> 01:30:10.590
The Kennedy campaign thought they had every hole plugged.

01:30:11.220 --> 01:30:12.910
John F. Kennedy.

01:30:14.340 --> 01:30:19.210
And aware that if something came unplugged,

01:30:19.980 --> 01:30:22.400
they wanted to be on top of it immediately.

01:30:23.200 --> 01:30:26.420
Senator Kennedy, one hundred and four and a half votes.

01:30:27.030 --> 01:30:29.540
And every delegation...

01:30:30.510 --> 01:30:31.450
was covered.

01:30:31.730 --> 01:30:36.150
The Wyoming vote will make the majority of Senator Kennedy.

01:30:39.840 --> 01:30:46.510
That the rules be suspended and that John F. Kennedy be nominated for President of the

01:30:46.510 --> 01:30:48.310
United States by acclamation.

01:31:12.960 --> 01:31:16.880
Next morning at 6.30, the phone rings in Bobby Kennedy's suite.

01:31:17.610 --> 01:31:18.370
It's his brother.

01:31:19.220 --> 01:31:26.440
He says, count up how many votes we have if we take the Northeast, the Eastern States, plus Texas.

01:31:27.260 --> 01:31:29.960
Bobby Kennedy calls in two of his...

01:31:30.000 --> 01:31:37.440
top advisers, Ken O'Donnell and Pierre Salinger. He says to them, count up these votes, plus Texas.

01:31:38.420 --> 01:31:43.600
Salinger, as he calls, says, you're not thinking of nominating Lyndon Johnson. You can't do that.

01:31:45.440 --> 01:31:52.720
Kennedy knew how Johnson talked about him. Little Johnny, or Sonny Boy, heard his pediatricians

01:31:52.720 --> 01:31:59.190
have given him a clean bill of health, and he knew his brother Bobby despised Johnson.

01:32:00.540 --> 01:32:03.960
But hatred was one of the few luxuries Kennedy could not afford,

01:32:04.980 --> 01:32:06.340
not in picking a running mate.

01:32:07.370 --> 01:32:10.570
The numbers said he needed to win Texas to win the presidency,

01:32:10.570 --> 01:32:13.820
and there was one man who could deliver the state.

01:32:16.210 --> 01:32:18.270
Robert Kennedy tried to stop it.

01:32:18.750 --> 01:32:22.670
He went down to try to persuade Johnson not to accept it,

01:32:23.420 --> 01:32:25.460
that the opposition to him was too great.

01:32:26.400 --> 01:32:29.860
I remember how haggard Bobby looked, Johnson obviously.

01:32:30.000 --> 01:32:34.000
They told him that he didn't want to speak to his brother's spokesman.

01:32:34.060 --> 01:32:35.200
He wanted to speak to his brother.

01:32:35.460 --> 01:32:37.500
If Jack had anything to say, he can call me.

01:32:37.600 --> 01:32:39.360
Here's my phone number.

01:32:39.920 --> 01:32:44.160
Senator Kennedy announced his choice as Senator Lyndon Baines Johnson

01:32:44.160 --> 01:32:47.200
of the state of Texas, the Senate majority leader,

01:32:47.360 --> 01:32:50.520
and his foremost rival for the presidential nomination.

01:32:50.970 --> 01:32:51.890
A rivalry which...

01:32:51.890 --> 01:32:53.810
There are many compartments in Jack's mind.

01:32:54.130 --> 01:32:56.370
I think the main one was that he wanted to win.

01:33:00.220 --> 01:33:05.700
candidate, Senator John Kennedy of Massachusetts, as he comes out of the Biltmore Hotel to come

01:33:05.700 --> 01:33:15.280
to his car. This motorcade will drive the three miles out here to the Coliseum. John F. Kennedy

01:33:15.280 --> 01:33:24.180
had never lost an election, and now, against all odds, at age 43, he was just one win away

01:33:24.180 --> 01:33:29.810
from the presidency. He was confident he could get there.

01:33:31.210 --> 01:33:36.040
What the American voters craved, Kennedy had come to understand, was a good story.

01:33:37.250 --> 01:33:41.180
And the set piece Kennedy would campaign on in the general election had it all.

01:33:42.400 --> 01:33:45.530
Good versus evil. Freedom versus slavery.

01:33:46.660 --> 01:33:50.860
A youthful paladin that would be himself and his powerful antagonist,

01:33:51.510 --> 01:33:56.760
the Soviet premier Nikita Khrushchev, who proved the perfect foil in 1960.

01:34:00.000 --> 01:34:08.420
world is changing the old era is ending the old ways will not do abroad the balance of power is

01:34:08.420 --> 01:34:15.990
shifting new and more terrible weapons are coming into use one third of the world may be free but

01:34:16.720 --> 01:34:23.420
one third is the victim of a cruel repression and the other third is rocked by poverty and hunger

01:34:23.420 --> 01:34:29.920
and disease communist influence has penetrated into asia it stands in the

01:34:30.000 --> 01:34:35.340
Middle East, some 90 miles off the coast of Florida.

01:34:35.980 --> 01:34:38.020
Khrushchev had come to the United States

01:34:38.020 --> 01:34:41.420
and the United Nations session in September of 1960,

01:34:41.700 --> 01:34:45.010
banged a shoe on the desk and said, we will bury you.

01:34:45.140 --> 01:34:47.720
We are grinding up missiles like sausages.

01:34:48.600 --> 01:34:51.100
So there was a heightened sense of competition.

01:34:51.260 --> 01:34:54.200
And this appealed to Kennedy's competitive spirit.

01:34:56.000 --> 01:34:58.780
And I say we can't afford to have the White House

01:34:58.780 --> 01:34:59.980
as a train-

01:35:01.840 --> 01:35:05.160
Kennedy was certain he could show himself the better man

01:35:05.340 --> 01:35:08.760
in a race against the sitting vice president, Richard Nixon.

01:35:19.430 --> 01:35:23.500
The polls, however, showed a dead heat coming out of the conventions

01:35:23.500 --> 01:35:27.520
and Kennedy could not shake free from the mire of religion.

01:35:30.000 --> 01:35:33.940
Watched with increasing ire as Protestant ministers across the country

01:35:33.940 --> 01:35:36.340
stirred opposition among their parishioners,

01:35:37.760 --> 01:35:43.520
the Reverend Martin Luther King, Sr. said he could not in good conscience vote for a Catholic.

01:35:44.230 --> 01:35:46.610
He instructed his flock to vote for Nixon.

01:35:47.880 --> 01:35:51.180
It was very nasty. I mean, let's just be blunt about it.

01:35:52.570 --> 01:35:54.990
For a while, he didn't really want to have to deal with it.

01:35:55.050 --> 01:35:59.350
He just wanted people to look at him and judge him on his own record.

01:36:01.240 --> 01:36:08.680
But it was getting so virulent and so scary that he then, in the fall campaign, went to Houston and spoke to the ministers.

01:36:08.900 --> 01:36:11.790
Went sort of into the belly of the beast, as it were.

01:36:16.150 --> 01:36:22.270
Reverend Rock, I'm grateful for your generous invitation to state my views.

01:36:23.020 --> 01:36:24.740
His advisor said, don't do this.

01:36:25.450 --> 01:36:27.890
You are just making religion an issue.

01:36:28.530 --> 01:36:29.970
You are actually speaking to the...

01:36:30.000 --> 01:36:34.960
bigots. The bigots want you to remind people that you're a Catholic, don't do this. He did it.

01:36:35.210 --> 01:36:41.130
So it is apparently necessary for me to state once again, not what kind of church I believe in,

01:36:41.860 --> 01:36:49.200
for that should be important only to me, but what kind of America I believe in. I believe in

01:36:49.200 --> 01:36:56.030
an America where the separation of church and state is absolute. He was making sort of a moral,

01:36:56.330 --> 01:36:59.450
ethical argument about what it means to be American.

01:37:00.760 --> 01:37:03.960
And this is the kind of America I fought for in the South Pacific

01:37:03.960 --> 01:37:06.760
and the kind my brother died for in Europe.

01:37:07.840 --> 01:37:11.080
No one suggested then that we might have a divided loyalty,

01:37:11.930 --> 01:37:13.450
that we did not believe in liberty,

01:37:14.420 --> 01:37:18.580
or that we belonged to a disloyal group that threatened, I quote,

01:37:18.760 --> 01:37:20.780
the freedom for which our forefathers died.

01:37:21.440 --> 01:37:25.390
And he said, we would hate to have a country that millions of people

01:37:25.390 --> 01:37:28.770
who on the day they're baptized are told they can't be president of the United States.

01:37:28.910 --> 01:37:29.970
So I want you to know...

01:37:30.000 --> 01:37:31.760
that I'm grateful to you for inviting me tonight.

01:37:31.760 --> 01:37:34.680
I'm sure that I have made no converts to my church,

01:37:34.700 --> 01:37:39.940
but I do hope, I do hope that at least my view,

01:37:40.120 --> 01:37:44.850
which I believe to be the view of my fellow Catholics

01:37:44.850 --> 01:37:47.370
who hold office, I hope that it may be of some value

01:37:47.370 --> 01:37:49.790
in at least assisting you to make a careful judgment.

01:38:06.440 --> 01:38:11.320
The general election campaign of 1960 featured a new wrinkle,

01:38:14.050 --> 01:38:18.070
the first-ever one-on-one debates between the major party candidates,

01:38:18.600 --> 01:38:21.290
broadcast live across the nation.

01:38:23.730 --> 01:38:28.710
In every campaign from 46 on,

01:38:29.120 --> 01:38:29.680
it was father.

01:38:30.000 --> 01:38:33.260
taught Jack how to use the camera as his friend,

01:38:34.060 --> 01:38:36.790
how to look into the camera, to smile,

01:38:37.230 --> 01:38:39.170
look charming, but be serious.

01:38:39.210 --> 01:38:43.340
It was no accident, no accident at all,

01:38:43.400 --> 01:38:46.520
that when Jack Kennedy debates Nixon,

01:38:47.110 --> 01:38:51.300
Nixon, the champion debater, comes off worse

01:38:51.390 --> 01:38:54.950
because Nixon doesn't know how to look into a camera.

01:38:55.300 --> 01:38:57.900
He doesn't know how to connect with an audience.

01:38:58.360 --> 01:38:59.960
He looks stiff, sweaty.

01:39:00.000 --> 01:39:01.220
Sweaty, scared.

01:39:02.160 --> 01:39:05.730
Jack is totally, absolutely composed.

01:39:06.840 --> 01:39:09.440
It isn't enough to compare what might have been done 8 years ago,

01:39:09.620 --> 01:39:12.000
or 10 years ago, or 15 years ago, or 20 years ago.

01:39:12.500 --> 01:39:14.360
I want to compare what we're doing with what...

01:39:14.360 --> 01:39:18.140
Nixon was someone who would sweat under the Klieg lights,

01:39:18.280 --> 01:39:22.940
and his makeup ran, and somebody later said

01:39:22.940 --> 01:39:25.580
he looked like a sinister chipmunk.

01:39:27.560 --> 01:39:29.970
See that in all the areas to which I...

01:39:31.210 --> 01:39:37.750
According to opinion polling, the majority of people who listened in on radio thought Nixon won the first debate.

01:39:41.570 --> 01:39:45.560
Among television viewers, the clear winner was Kennedy.

01:39:48.880 --> 01:39:53.290
The split decision in the opening debate was a wake-up call.

01:39:54.260 --> 01:39:59.970
And through the next three debates, and every day in between, Kennedy kept hammering at Eisenhower.

01:40:07.750 --> 01:40:14.620
and Nixon. He hit them for allowing Americans to lose their sense of national purpose, for

01:40:14.620 --> 01:40:21.110
allowing the US economic engine to sputter as compared to the Soviets, for allowing the United

01:40:21.110 --> 01:40:27.860
States to fall behind the Soviet Union in science and technology, and most dangerously in nuclear

01:40:27.860 --> 01:40:29.980
clear arms, what Kennedy called

01:40:30.000 --> 01:40:30.960
the Missile Gap.

01:40:31.520 --> 01:40:33.240
If there is any lesson of the summit,

01:40:34.130 --> 01:40:35.670
it is that the communists believe

01:40:35.670 --> 01:40:37.450
that the military balance of power

01:40:37.450 --> 01:40:39.300
is shifting in their direction.

01:40:39.440 --> 01:40:41.040
Why was the United States unable

01:40:41.040 --> 01:40:43.180
to get an indictment of Casco by name?

01:40:43.520 --> 01:40:46.300
Jack was the representative

01:40:46.300 --> 01:40:50.580
of the new, young, vibrant generation.

01:40:51.340 --> 01:40:55.220
And Jack ran on that theme and ran hard.

01:40:55.760 --> 01:40:57.400
The United States looks tired.

01:40:58.170 --> 01:40:59.970
It looks like our brightest days have been...

01:41:00.000 --> 01:41:04.040
It looks like the communists are reaching for the future,

01:41:04.060 --> 01:41:08.600
and we sit back and talk about the ideals of the American Revolution.

01:41:09.470 --> 01:41:15.500
He says Eisenhower was an old man who wasn't watching over the store anymore,

01:41:15.500 --> 01:41:17.740
and Nixon was his accomplice.

01:41:18.010 --> 01:41:24.740
And only this young hero, Jack Kennedy, who knew how to fight, knew how to win,

01:41:24.930 --> 01:41:28.330
was going to put the United States back in its commanding position again.

01:41:28.810 --> 01:41:29.930
And there's a direct line.

01:41:30.000 --> 01:41:34.280
between what Jack says in 1960 and what he writes in 1939,

01:41:34.750 --> 01:41:35.710
why England slept.

01:41:36.530 --> 01:41:39.370
The only way to deter aggression

01:41:39.840 --> 01:41:44.640
is to have an impregnable military defense.

01:41:44.960 --> 01:41:47.940
And I'm the one who can build that military

01:41:47.940 --> 01:41:50.290
because I'm the new man,

01:41:50.490 --> 01:41:52.450
the man of the future, the new generation,

01:41:53.050 --> 01:41:54.450
not an old, tired Republican.

01:42:00.120 --> 01:42:03.540
Kennedy opened a comfortable lead in the polls in mid-October,

01:42:04.440 --> 01:42:08.580
but he was careful not to get swept up in the energy and excitement of his rallies.

01:42:10.800 --> 01:42:12.960
The Catholic questions still worried him,

01:42:13.840 --> 01:42:17.480
and the issue of civil rights demanded cunning political calculation.

01:42:19.450 --> 01:42:22.310
He placated white supremacist Democrats in the South,

01:42:22.390 --> 01:42:26.610
who insisted on their right to enforce segregation in their own states.

01:42:27.960 --> 01:42:29.940
But he also meant to signal his sympathy.

01:42:30.000 --> 01:42:32.840
to the growing number of African-American voters,

01:42:33.280 --> 01:42:34.520
North and South.

01:42:36.550 --> 01:42:39.450
At that time, the 1960s,

01:42:40.120 --> 01:42:42.580
the black community across the South

01:42:42.580 --> 01:42:45.690
were largely Abraham Lincoln Republicans.

01:42:46.700 --> 01:42:48.320
My parents were Republicans.

01:42:49.160 --> 01:42:54.440
And I was rather cynical about the Kennedy family

01:42:54.810 --> 01:42:58.090
that they didn't know any black people.

01:42:58.990 --> 01:42:59.970
There was a deep sea...

01:43:00.000 --> 01:43:06.800
did personal suffering that we didn't own in rural South, but Kennedy didn't know any of that.

01:43:09.080 --> 01:43:14.560
Kennedy shadowed Nixon's position on civil rights. Both candidates talked of promoting

01:43:14.560 --> 01:43:20.500
equal opportunity for everyone, but neither was willing to pledge federal power to actually

01:43:20.500 --> 01:43:27.640
enforce court-ordered integration of schools and public accommodations. As the campaign

01:43:27.640 --> 01:43:29.220
headed into its final days, though,

01:43:30.000 --> 01:43:32.760
found a way to separate himself from Nixon.

01:43:33.680 --> 01:43:35.460
Dr. Martin Luther King, Jr.,

01:43:35.770 --> 01:43:38.090
the nation's most respected civil rights leader,

01:43:38.630 --> 01:43:40.590
was arrested at a protest in Georgia.

01:43:42.720 --> 01:43:45.920
They put him in chains and put him in the back of a paddy wagon

01:43:46.420 --> 01:43:51.550
and drove him 300 miles south to Reidsville Penitentiary

01:43:51.890 --> 01:43:55.290
in the middle of the night, and nobody knew where he was.

01:43:57.100 --> 01:43:59.970
Coretta King was six months pregnant, and...

01:44:00.000 --> 01:44:06.330
And I had never seen her panic, but she was panicked by this and called me and said,

01:44:06.430 --> 01:44:10.590
I think they're going to kill him and, you know, can't you do anything?

01:44:12.170 --> 01:44:18.810
Kennedy's brother-in-law, Sergeant Shriver, went to see if the candidate might be willing to reach out to Dr. King's wife.

01:44:19.910 --> 01:44:27.310
Shriver knew the Kennedy political operation wanted no part of a civil rights controversy in the final days of the campaign.

01:44:28.240 --> 01:44:29.980
He said, you know, you've been trying to figure...

01:44:30.000 --> 01:44:35.320
out what you could do that would help in this situation and you can't issue a public statement

01:44:35.320 --> 01:44:41.880
but what about calling her and conveying your sympathy he said kennedy thought for a couple of

01:44:41.880 --> 01:44:48.230
minutes and then a good kennedy grin said that's a very good idea do you have her number on the

01:44:48.230 --> 01:44:54.570
airplane salinger asked kennedy what did you do anything when we were all out and he said yeah i

01:44:54.570 --> 01:44:59.140
called mrs martin luther king and they went wild and bobby was just

01:45:00.000 --> 01:45:06.330
livid with anger and fury and fear that it was going to, you know, lose a number of southern

01:45:06.330 --> 01:45:14.720
states. Bobby eventually calmed down and made a series of discreet phone calls to help free King,

01:45:16.300 --> 01:45:21.990
but Kennedy's team kept most of the maneuvering under wraps, and they did not talk up the call

01:45:21.990 --> 01:45:28.140
to Coretta to the national press. They were, however, quick to take advantage when Mrs. King

01:45:28.140 --> 01:45:29.970
went public about her sympathetic...

01:45:30.000 --> 01:45:37.180
call from Kennedy. The campaign printed hundreds of thousands of pamphlets telling the story of

01:45:37.180 --> 01:45:43.190
Kennedy's kindness to the King family and Nixon's silence and shipped those pamphlets across the

01:45:43.190 --> 01:45:50.580
country, many by Greyhound bus to be distributed at black churches. The reaction that got the

01:45:50.580 --> 01:45:58.470
publicity was Daddy King saying, I got a whole suitcase full of votes and I'm gonna throw them

01:45:58.470 --> 01:45:59.690
toward this Kennedy boy.

01:46:00.380 --> 01:46:06.360
I wasn't sure about a Catholic in the White House, but he's won me over.

01:46:17.950 --> 01:46:27.090
In the final push of the campaign, the crowds that came out were the biggest Kennedy had ever seen.

01:46:28.970 --> 01:46:29.970
But the candidate...

01:46:30.000 --> 01:46:40.490
spent and edgy he didn't like the feel of the race on the eve of the election he was sure

01:46:40.490 --> 01:46:46.840
nixon was closing on him he wanted to fly west for a little extra campaigning his advisors

01:46:46.840 --> 01:46:52.660
insisted there was little left to do and so kennedy settled in at the family compound in

01:46:52.660 --> 01:46:55.290
hyannisport to watch the results come in

01:47:04.190 --> 01:47:09.120
The house next door, which was Bobby's house, was set up as campaign headquarters,

01:47:09.240 --> 01:47:13.940
and all the children's bedrooms were turned into research rooms.

01:47:16.040 --> 01:47:21.030
As the returns came in, it was frighteningly close.

01:47:21.580 --> 01:47:28.230
There was a problem with the Catholic vote, which they had hoped would be 90 percent, was 80 percent.

01:47:30.000 --> 01:47:37.680
But worse, traditional Democratic votes in Protestant areas were not coming in Democratic.

01:47:38.940 --> 01:47:43.520
Protestants weren't voting for Jack Kennedy, they were either not voting or they were voting

01:47:43.520 --> 01:47:44.000
for Nixon.

01:47:44.730 --> 01:47:50.050
We're trying to settle here so far without any success, or without enough success, closest,

01:47:50.370 --> 01:47:53.530
one of the closest elections in the history of the United States.

01:47:54.320 --> 01:47:59.970
And so we just waited, nobody could eat much, and the calls were coming.

01:48:00.000 --> 01:48:06.860
in from various states. Jack was over at his house, and Bobby would keep in touch with him.

01:48:07.600 --> 01:48:11.900
That's the way it lasted through the night. I finally went to bed. It still hadn't been decided.

01:48:13.620 --> 01:48:17.440
Kennedy didn't know if he'd won or not when he went to sleep that night.

01:48:18.590 --> 01:48:22.770
The press was unable to make sense of the vote totals out of Cook County, Illinois.

01:48:24.170 --> 01:48:27.170
We'll have some states that are uncertain, but I have my...

01:48:27.170 --> 01:48:28.730
Texas was neck and neck.

01:48:30.000 --> 01:48:32.000
Nobody could call California.

01:48:33.350 --> 01:48:38.110
So it's 6 a.m. in New York, and I don't know how long we'll be here.

01:48:38.190 --> 01:48:39.090
Nobody's told us yet.

01:48:39.520 --> 01:48:43.760
Ray Scherer, NBC's campaign and election reporter,

01:48:43.920 --> 01:48:46.920
is now standing by at Hyannis, Massachusetts.

01:48:47.400 --> 01:48:49.160
Is Senator Kennedy asleep, do you know?

01:48:49.480 --> 01:48:51.940
He's asleep, Dave. He's going to rise at 9.15.

01:48:51.940 --> 01:48:55.280
He got in the sack about 4.30. That'll give him about five hours.

01:48:57.240 --> 01:48:58.480
Are you going to check the thing to see?

01:49:00.000 --> 01:49:02.360
and maybe there won't,

01:49:02.580 --> 01:49:04.720
in which case you fellas left staying on the air all day.

01:49:06.340 --> 01:49:08.160
When he did wake up the next morning,

01:49:08.880 --> 01:49:10.920
Jack Kennedy was president-elect.

01:49:12.000 --> 01:49:13.520
He had won the popular vote

01:49:13.520 --> 01:49:16.480
by less than one-quarter of one percent.

01:49:17.970 --> 01:49:20.900
It was very nerve-wracking, and then it was done.

01:49:21.920 --> 01:49:23.520
So we went out and played touch football,

01:49:24.040 --> 01:49:26.080
and my father came out and said,

01:49:26.280 --> 01:49:29.980
it's time for lunch, and whenever he...

01:49:30.160 --> 01:49:31.620
You know, what did he got?

01:49:32.160 --> 01:49:32.640
Immediately.

01:49:33.260 --> 01:49:34.860
He was a stickler for time.

01:49:36.270 --> 01:49:37.950
Jack and I were the last ones to go up.

01:49:38.090 --> 01:49:39.130
And he turned to me and said,

01:49:39.960 --> 01:49:41.860
doesn't he know I'm president of the United States?

01:49:42.080 --> 01:49:44.440
And I thought, that's a perfect ending to a day.

01:49:47.200 --> 01:49:49.720
Kennedy's razor-thin advantages in Illinois

01:49:49.720 --> 01:49:51.390
and 10 other states

01:49:51.390 --> 01:49:53.930
had made the difference in electoral votes.

01:49:55.320 --> 01:49:57.660
His huge margin among black voters

01:49:57.660 --> 01:49:59.980
helped pull him through in as many...

01:50:00.000 --> 01:50:01.780
as five of those key states.

01:50:02.700 --> 01:50:05.500
And the bet on Lyndon Johnson had paid off.

01:50:06.740 --> 01:50:09.380
The Kennedy-Johnson ticket had carried Texas.

01:50:09.580 --> 01:50:16.210
And there he is, the next president of the United States.

01:50:17.350 --> 01:50:18.870
He always sits in the front seat,

01:50:19.030 --> 01:50:21.350
and incidentally, so does Mr. Khrushchev.

01:50:21.450 --> 01:50:23.610
These people find that you can wave more easily

01:50:23.610 --> 01:50:24.310
from that point.

01:50:25.690 --> 01:50:29.980
And I can assure you that every degree of mind is...

01:50:30.030 --> 01:50:35.300
The spirit that I possess will be devoted to the long-range interests of the United States

01:50:36.060 --> 01:50:38.300
and to the cause of freedom around the world.

01:50:40.310 --> 01:50:45.890
So now my wife and I prepare for a new administration and for a new baby.

01:50:46.310 --> 01:50:46.650
Thank you.

01:50:51.960 --> 01:50:55.440
John F. Kennedy had spent the campaign of 1960

01:50:55.440 --> 01:50:59.220
telling the American people he would be a new kind of president.

01:51:00.410 --> 01:51:03.020
He'd promised not just dynamism, but strength.

01:51:04.830 --> 01:51:06.950
He had promised to stand up to the Soviets

01:51:06.950 --> 01:51:10.070
and to protect American preeminence in the world.

01:51:12.330 --> 01:51:15.110
His stubborn insistence on being the kind of leader

01:51:15.110 --> 01:51:17.940
he'd vowed to be would make his presidency

01:51:17.940 --> 01:51:21.820
among the most energetic, the most far-reaching,

01:51:22.690 --> 01:51:27.200
the most perilous, and the most tragic in American history.

01:52:30.490 --> 01:52:30.550
Thank you.

